{-
   What a **declaration path** cannot name, each refused by name -
   `doc/inflight/Design-Iterator-Objects.md` §2.4 and §6.5.

   A path is `f.Iter` or `f.Result` and nothing else, and what it reaches has to be a declaration
   that publishes the one the author wrote. Three families here:

   | | |
   | --- | --- |
   | the head | a name nothing declares, a global, a producer another module keeps private |
   | the member | a name that is neither, `.Iter` at a function that is not an `iter fn`, `.Result` at one that is |
   | the position | an alias target and a constant, both read before any declaration has a body |

   The **position** family is the one worth reading. A site type is minted out of a producer's own
   MIR, so a path can only be resolved once every declaration is a name with a signature behind it -
   which is what a signature and a body have and what a record's fields and an alias target do not.
   A `data` is answered by reading its fields a second time after the declaration pass, which is why
   `iter/Name.yana`'s `Parser` compiles; an alias has no such pass and says so.
-}

import Native
import Lexing

iter fn lex(limit: I32) -> I32:
    let &at = 0 :: I32

    while at < limit:
        yield at
        at = at + 1

iter fn (TrivialCopy(a)) twice(x: a) -> a:
    yield x
    yield x

fn plain(x: I32) = x + 1

lens fn around():
    yield {}

let counter = 4

-- An **alias target**, which is read one phase before any function is declared. The `data` beside it
-- is answered by a second pass and this is not: an alias stands for its target everywhere the target
-- could stand, so deferring one would defer every position that names it.
alias Tokens = lex.Iter

{-
   §6.5 - a site type's size in a **constant** position.

   The record's layout is a fact about a body, and a constant is settled before any body is read. So
   the answer would depend on which declaration the pass happened to reach first, which is exactly
   the kind of order dependence this stage refuses rather than resolves. `iter/Name.yana`'s
   `measured` is the position that works.
-}
data Padded {pad: [U8 *(traits() :: TypeTraits(lex.Iter)).size]}

{-
   A producer that captured a loan publishes an **origin**, and a record holding one has to say
   whose storage it is - which is the rule for a field of any borrowed type and not one of this
   feature's. `iter/Name.yana`'s `Scan` writes `src'advancing.Iter` and is accepted.
-}
data Counter {at: I32}

iter fn advancing(&c: Counter, limit: I32) -> I32:
    while c.at < limit:
        yield c.at
        c.at = c.at + 1

data Scan {cursor: advancing.Iter}

-- The head: a name nothing declares, and one that is a global rather than a function.
fn missing(v: nowhere.Iter) -> I32 = 0
fn global(v: counter.Iter) -> I32 = 0

-- And one another module declares without `pub`. A path is a *use* of a name, so it is the same
-- "not in scope" a call of it would be - `Lexing.digits` beside it is exported and works.
fn hidden(v: Lexing.private.Iter) -> I32 = 0

-- The member: neither of the two a declaration publishes.
fn wrongMember(v: lex.Thing) -> I32 = 0

-- `.Iter` at a function that has no object type. The message names what the declaration *is*,
-- because a plain function and a `lens fn` are fixed differently.
fn notIter(v: plain.Iter) -> I32 = 0
fn lensIter(v: around.Iter) -> I32 = 0

-- And `.Result` at an `iter fn`, whose result is the exit signal rather than what it hands over.
fn iterResult(v: lex.Result) -> I32 = 0

-- `.Result` at a function whose result has a name of its own. A path to `I32` would be a second
-- spelling for a type that already has one.
fn plainResult(v: plain.Result) -> I32 = 0

-- The arguments, which bind the declaration's own type parameters.
fn tooMany(v: lex.Iter(I32)) -> I32 = 0
fn tooFew(v: twice.Iter) -> I32 = 0

{-
   A path at a variable the enclosing declaration binds.

   A site type has no parameters: it is minted from one producer read under one substitution, and a
   body compiled once has no substitution to mint it under. The same sentence `let it = f(...)`
   inside a generic body gets (`Reject.Object.yana`), said about the type rather than about the
   construction.
-}
fn (a) inGeneric(v: twice.Iter(a)) -> I32 = 0

{-
   And one row that is about **what else this module reports**, not about a path at all.

   A module holding a path runs the declaration groups early, so that the bodies a path demands see
   the same program every other body does - and a global's initializer is code, which was being run
   against declarations that had already failed. `Broken` is refused for its field type and then
   `Broken {slot: 1}` was refused a second time for having no field of that name, which is a sentence
   about a declaration that plainly writes one. The list beside this file is the assertion: one error
   here and not two.

   Written in this file because the trigger is the path above it - the same module without one stops
   at the checkpoint and never resolves the initializer at all, which is the behaviour this restores.
-}
data Broken {slot: NoSuchType}

let &initialized = Broken {slot: 1}

fn main() -> I32 = 0
